    <item>
      <title>Wine-Staging 11.18 Adds New Patches To Further Improve WoW64</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-Staging-11.18</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-Staging-11.18</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 13:22:39 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="WINE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/wine.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Building off yesterday's &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Wine-11.18-Released"&gt;Wine 11.18&lt;/a&gt; bi-weekly development release is now Wine-Staging 11.18. This experimental flavor of Wine is presently carrying 273 extra patches over that upstream Git state, including some new patches for helping enhance the WoW64 support. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine-Staging 11.18 includes some new WoW64 patches to check the binary before setting the WoW64 entry as well as for not writing WoW64 for 64-bit binaries. Wine WoW64 as a reminder is the means of running 32-bit Windows applications inside a pure 64-bit environment. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;One of the fixes with these latest WoW64 patches in staging is for &lt;a href="https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=47175"&gt;this bug from 2019&lt;/a&gt; around the kernel services code. There is similarly &lt;a href="https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=50431"&gt;this bug report&lt;/a&gt; from 2020 also in the WoW64 services code. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine-Staging 11.18 also carries new patches for converting YV12 to RGB on render in the Quartz code for &lt;a href="https://bugs.winehq.org/show_bug.cgi?id=59846"&gt;this bug&lt;/a&gt; over Worms Forts: Under Siege (GOG.com) crashing when trying to play the intro. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Aside from that work, Wine-Staging 11.18 also pulls in the latest VKD3D Git code. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Wine-Staging 11.18 and Wine 11.18 are both available from &lt;a href="https://www.winehq.org/"&gt;WineHQ.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GNOME OS Turns To Zswap To Deal With OOM Issues, Progress On Geary Email Client Fork</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-OS-Goes-Zswap</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-OS-Goes-Zswap</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:38:08 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; In addition to this week's &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-51-Released"&gt;GNOME 51 release&lt;/a&gt; were several other exciting developments in the GNOME desktop space. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For GNOME OS is now a &lt;a href="https://gnome.pages.gitlab.gnome.org/gnome-build-meta/docs/index.html"&gt;documentation website&lt;/a&gt; with guides and other information to help users in trying out GNOME OS and carryout related setup. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GNOME OS is also now employing Zswap to help with a fast and responsive system even if your system doesn't have too much memory.  This change should help those that have experienced out-of-memory issues on GNOME OS with Zswap set to be used by default. GNOME OS is relying on a Rust-based zswap-gnerator script to create the proper systemd configuration for Zswap. Zswap-generator will respect &lt;em&gt;zswap.enabled=0&lt;/em&gt; on the kernel command-line for disabling the functionality. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=convey" alt="Convey" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;In third-party GNOME app news, Convey has debuted on &lt;a href="https://flathub.org/en/apps/net.donnybeelo.Convey"&gt;Flathub&lt;/a&gt; as a fork of the Geary mail client. Convey forked Geary and added Microsoft 365 support, ported to the GTK4 toolkit, and brings other fixes and UI/UX enhancements. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these interesting GNOME developments for the week via &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/09/twig-266/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Intel Xe Driver For Linux 7.4 Adds vRAM Health Check, Degraded Memory Handling</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Xe-vRAM-Health-Check</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-Xe-vRAM-Health-Check</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:24:15 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="INTEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/intel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Sent out this week were more Intel Xe kernel graphics driver changes targeting the upcoming &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/Linux+7.4"&gt;Linux 7.4&lt;/a&gt; kernel cycle. The theme this week was around dealing with video memory health and also being able to better deal with hardware degraded video RAM. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Intel Xe driver is rolling out a basic vRAM health check routine focused on sanity testing the last vRAM page. Intel engineer Matthew Auld explained of this vRAM health check for the Xe driver with &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260902124117.918018-12-matthew.auld@intel.com/"&gt;this patch&lt;/a&gt;: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"During early probe, use the last page as a canary for BAR sizing, CCS sizing, identity map setup etc. If something is wrong the last page is where we will likely find it. Hit it with everything we have. For now this is gated behind a debug config option, so shouldn't trigger on production. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Main motivation is around CCS sizing where on some BMG cards the CCS offset is programmed misaligned, for whatever reason, and our handling of that was busted, as found by Linus, leading to some amount of CCS storage getting pulled into the allocator as normal VRAM."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For now at least this vRAM health check option is gated by the &lt;em&gt;CONFIG_DRM_XE_DEBUG_MEM&lt;/em&gt; kernel build flag. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=linux-71-arc-pro-b70&amp;image=linux_71_b70_1_med" alt="Intel Arc Pro Linux testing" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Separately this week's Xe pull request also brings in memory page offlining support after going through 21 rounds of review. This is a "significant step" toward making the Xe kernel driver gracefully handle hardware memory degradation. With this work the Xe driver can permanently carve out faulty video memory so it isn't reused by subsequent allocations. The bad vRAM pages are also exposed via DebugFS for user-space to be aware of the faulty memory areas. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Intel Xe driver with these newest queued changes also now exposes per-engine idle status via DebugFS.  &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of Intel Xe kernel driver changes submitted this week to DRM-Next for Linux 7.4 can be found via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260903161553.528932-17-tejas.upadhyay@intel.com/"&gt;this pull&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Seeing More Polishing &amp; Fixes, Plasma 6.9 Begins Seeing More Activity</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Plasma-6.8-Post-Beta-Fixes</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Plasma-6.8-Post-Beta-Fixes</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:10:34 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Following &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Beta"&gt;last week's Plasma 6.8 beta release&lt;/a&gt;, KDE desktop developers have spent this week mostly working on bug fixes as well as some early changes toward Plasma 6.9. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 beta appears to be doing fairly well in testing with &lt;a href="https://bugs.kde.org/buglist.cgi?bug_status=UNCONFIRMED&amp;bug_status=CONFIRMED&amp;bug_status=ASSIGNED&amp;bug_status=REOPENED&amp;f1=classification&amp;keywords=regression&amp;keywords_type=allwords&amp;known_name=Plasma%3A%206.7%20regressions&amp;list_id=3592308&amp;o1=notequals&amp;query_based_on=Plasma%3A%206.7%20regressions&amp;query_format=advanced&amp;v1=Frameworks%20And%20Libraries&amp;version=6.7.80&amp;version=6.7.90"&gt;the regression list&lt;/a&gt; currently at just five bugs. Those bugs pertain to the logout screen losing focus, misaligned notifications, misaligned logout/restart/shutdown overlay in multi-monitor setups, and "Switch User" behavior. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As for some of this week's KDE Plasma changes: &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;With Plasma 6.8 is the new Kup backup solution and as of this week it will now show the elapsed time and the average speed information is more accurate. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The KDE desktop portal dialogs have also been more polished for Plasma 6.8. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 will also no longer write to a cache file every time it displays a notification. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Plasma 6.7.6 is a bug fix where KWin could occasionally crash when returning to a game using Alt + Tab. For Plasma 6.8 is a KWin crash fix when interacting with titlebar buttons in very specific ways. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Meanwhile for Plasma 6.9, when renaming a file or folder on the desktop now all the standard warnings and confirmations will be shown when necessary. This is for matters like hiding a file/folder warning or when changing the file type/extension. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kde_file_warning_med" alt="KDE file warning" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on the KDE Plasma changes made in recent days via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/09/19/this-week-in-plasma-let-the-polishing-begin/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
